def point_in_triangle(p, a, b, c):
    def cross(o, a, b):
        return (a[0] - o[0]) * (b[1] - o[1]) - (a[1] - o[1]) * (b[0] - o[0])
    c1 = cross(a, b, p)
    c2 = cross(b, c, p)
    c3 = cross(c, a, p)
    # Non-degenerate triangle: a, b, c are not colinear, so signs 0 happens only on edges
    return (c1 >= 0 and c2 >= 0 and c3 >= 0) or (c1 <= 0 and c2 <= 0 and c3 <= 0)
```

The test is a three-dimensional version of barycentric coordinates. I compute the signed area of each of the three sub-triangles formed by the test point and one side. The point lies within the triangle, including on its edges, if the three sub-areas are all positive or all negative, since that corresponds to the point being on the same side of each edge as the opposite vertex. The triangle is assumed non-degenerate, so one area cannot be exactly zero unless the point lies on an edge, and that case is covered by the second condition.

