The function determines whether a polygon is convex by examining the sign of cross products. For each vertex it computes the cross product of the edge from the current vertex to the next, and the vector from that vertex to the one after the next. If the cross product is positive, the turn is leftward; if it is negative, the turn is rightward; and a zero product indicates three collinear points.

Consecutive edges must not turn in opposite directions. Collinearity of three points also disqualifies the polygon because the definition requires strictly alternating left and right turns. I close the polygon by repeating the first point, so the last turn compares the final edge with the closed first edge. If any cross product changes sign, the vertices have a reflex angle, and the polygon is not convex. Exactly three collinear points means at least one cross product is zero, so the function rejects degenerate straight lines as well.

