                return False
        return True
    return True
```

The function determines whether a polygon is convex by examining the sign of every pair of adjacent clockwise edges. For each vertex, it computes the cross product of the vector from that vertex to its successor with the vector from that successor to its successor, which gives the signed area of the triangle formed by the three points. The polygon is convex only when all cross products have the same sign. At the same time, the sign of any two consecutive cross products must agree across the three-point window they share, so the sign test must be applied simultaneously rather than globally. Whenever any cross product is zero, the three points lie on a straight line and the polygon is not convex under the rule in the assertion. The loop returns False as soon as either digression is found, and True only when no such situation occurs. Because a single zero is enough to reject a side, and because two consecutive near-zero products that disagree can be detected only when adjacent, both conditions cover the examples.

