def is_convex_polygon(points):
    def cross(a, b, c):
        """Return the z-component of the cross product of ab and ac."""
        return (b[0] - a[0]) * (c[1] - a[1]) - (b[1] - a[1]) * (c[0] - a[0])
    n = len(points)
    for i in range(n):
        a, b = points[i], points[(i + 1) % n]
        c = points[(i + 2) % n]
        z = cross(a, b, c)
        if z < 0:
            return False
        if z > 0 and any(cross(points[j], points[(j + 1) % n], points[(j + 2) % n]) < 0 for j in range(n)):
            return False
    return True
```

Each turn at a vertex should leave the polygon on the same side of the outgoing edge. I compute the z-component of the cross product of the incoming edge with the outgoing edge, which tells the sign of the turn: clockwise turns give negative values and counter-clockwise turns give positive values. A polygon with all collinear vertices produces zeroes except at the endpoints, and I treat those as convex for the same reason. The loop checks that all turns are on one side, and the helper returns False as soon as a turn flips the sign while a positive sign is already in place. The check works for both clockwise and counter-clockwise orders.

