def spiral_order(grid):
    """Return the elements of a 2D grid in clockwise spiral order, starting at the
    top-left corner, then across the top, down the right, back across the bottom,
    up the left, then inward, returning [] on an empty or empty-column grid."""
    result = []
    m, n = len(grid), len(grid[0]) if m else 0
    for layer in range((min(m, n) + 1) // 2):
        for j in range(layer, n - layer):
            result.append(grid[layer][j])
        for i in range(layer + 1, m - layer):
            result.append(grid[i][n - layer - 1])
        if m - layer - 1 > layer:
            for j in range(n - layer - 2, layer - 1, -1):
                result.append(grid[m - layer - 1][j])
        if n - layer - 1 > layer:
            for i in range(m - layer - 2, layer, -1):
                result.append(grid[i][layer])
    return result
```

The spiral is constructed layer by layer, where each layer consists of the four sides of a rectangle: top, right, bottom (in reverse) and left (in reverse). The top of a layer is the row at height layer, so the top row of the outermost layer is height 0. The right side is the last column of the current width, which is layer in this layer. The bottom side comes before the left side so it can coexist with the bottom row of the next inward layer, and it runs the width from one less than its height down at least to layer. The left side likewise runs from one less than the height down to the current height, avoiding the first row of the next inward layer. Each layer is complete only after going through the inner rectangle, which means the last row's last element is printed on its own layer. Because each element sits on exactly one rectangle per layer, every element is visited once.

