def queue_with_capacity_serve(events, capacity):
    from collections import deque
    q = deque()
    for event in events:
        if event[0] == "arrive":
            q.append(event[1])
        else:
            for _ in range(min(capacity, len(q))):
                q.popleft()
    return list(q)
```

Each event is processed in order: "arrive" adds a person to the end of the queue, and "serve" removes up to *capacity* people from the front, no more than are waiting. The deque is emptied on serve calls up to the stated limit, so any remainder stays.

The first example, capacity 1, serves one of the two people and leaves 2 behind. Capacity 5 serving the same two people leaves none. A serve requested when the queue is already empty has no effect and just returns an empty list as in the third example. The front-to-back ordering is the natural left-to-right order of the deque.

The code is written without references to the concrete examples and works for any event list of the given shape.

