def parse_env_line(line):
    equals = line.find('=')
    key = line[:equals]
    rest = line[equals + 1:]
    if len(rest) >= 2:
        first, last = rest[0], rest[-1]
        if first == last and (first == '"' or first == "'"):
            return (key, rest[1:-1])
    if '=' in rest:
        rest = rest[:rest.find('=')]
    return (key, rest)
```

The first cutoff splits the line into key and the value plus any trailing equals sign. The second split strips quotes only when the value part starts and ends with the same quote character and has at least two characters, removing the quote characters. A second equals in the value part is not a separator, and any value part that contains an equals sign is truncated at its first one, capturing the usual quoted forms. Everything else is kept as written, and the key is guaranteed not to contain an equals sign because it stops before a separator. Because the fallback returns the entire remaining part, a trailing equals sign with nothing after it is not an error. Only one line at a time is parsed, so no state is carried between lines.

